# Rock candy: why the sugar comes back, and why it waits to be asked
#
# Hot water holds far more sugar than cold water does. Dissolve as much as
# hot water will take, let it cool, and the water is now holding more than it
# can — but it does not let go until something gives the first crystal
# somewhere to start. That is why the experiment needs a string, and why the
# jar can sit clear on a shelf for a day and then turn solid in a minute.
register lv1

# 1. Cold water first, so the difference has something to be measured against.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 table_sugar 300g
inspect v1

# 2. The same sugar into hot water. Far more of it goes in.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL @ 95C
add v2 table_sugar 300g
inspect v2

# 3. Cool the hot one. Nothing comes out — and the bench says why rather
#    than quietly crystallising it, because a syrup that crystallises on its
#    own is not the experiment.
cool v2 30kJ
look v2
inspect v2

# 4. Now give it a seed: one crystal of the same sugar is all it takes.
add v2 table_sugar 1g
look v2
inspect v2

# The solution ends at exactly the limit for its temperature, and everything
# above that is now crystal. That is the yield of the experiment, and nobody
# had to be told it — it is the difference between two solubilities.

# What this does not model: the limit is a straight line between reviewed
# values at 20 °C and 100 °C, so the middle of the range is a few percent
# low; crystals grow instantly rather than over days, with no size, habit or
# purity; and nothing here nucleates spontaneously, so a real syrup's
# unpredictability — the reason one jar sets overnight and the next does not —
# is outside this model.
